  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nAn Omega block that is in place over the CONUS is expected to \npersist through the end of the week. The associated ridge axis \nwill only ever so slowly crawl eastward over the coming days, \nwhich will maintain broad surface high pressure across the \nregion. As a result, dry conditions with an abundance of \nsunshine will continue through the remainder of the week with \nhigh temperatures remaining above to well above normal for this\ntime of year. Daily lake breezes will cause the coolest (low-\nmid 60s) high temperatures to be observed near Lake Michigan\neach day.\n\nMuch like last night/early this morning, optimal radiational \ncooling conditions will likely allow for fog to develop late \ntonight into tomorrow morning and possibly again in the coming \nnights/mornings. This will particularly be the case in and near\nthe Chicago metro within the footprint of the marine-influenced\nair brought inland during the daytime, where the fog could \nbecome dense once again.\n\nThere is pretty good ensemble agreement at this point in time \nthat the blocking pattern will break down by early next week. \nThis will allow for a cold front to drop through the region \nsometime on Monday or Tuesday, resulting in a return to cooler \nand more seasonable temperatures for the middle of next week. \nThe frontal passage may bring a chance for precipitation to the \narea early-mid next week, but support for this across the \nlatest suite of ensemble guidance is not ubiquitous, and there \nare considerable temporal differences with when this next \npotential opportunity for precipitation may arrive as well.\n\nOgorek",
